3.	Reference
TD SYNNEX has found that the data in ACM is generally within 3-5% of the invoice.  There are a few reasons that the costs do not align including:
3.1.	Estimates vs Actuals
Azure Cost Management provides estimated usage and cost data, which may not reflect final pricing, discounts, taxes, or currency conversions applied in the actual invoice.
3.2.	Timing
Cost Management data is often near real-time or daily, whereas the Partner Center reconciliation file contains finalized monthly billing data that aligns with your invoice.
3.3.	Volume Pricing
Some resources have some amount of free consumption after which there are additional charges.  Additionally, some charges may decrease as volume increases.
3.4.	Missing Financial Details
ACM does not include tax calculations, partner markup or discounts, and credits or adjustments.
3.5.	Missing Charges
ACM is not guaranteed to include all charges for resources at a specific point in time.
3.6.	Non-Azure Resources
ACM may include costs for resources not in Azure such as Modern Worlplace items.
3.7.	Partner or 3rd Party Services
ACM will not include charges from partners or 3rd parties for professional / managed services or other products that they may have included in their invoice.
3.8.	Costs from Prior Periods
Microsoft may invoice some costs from prior periods in the current invoice.  These costs would not be reflected in ACM for the current month.

4.	Definitions
Term	Definition
ACM	Azure Cost Management is a native tool within the Azure portal that helps users monitor and optimize cloud spending across Azure services.
MCM	Microsoft Cost Management is often used interchangeably with ACM.  It includes data from ACM but also includes Microsoft 365 and has APIs for programmatic access.
